{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-12 12:59:52 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-12 07:59:52 America/New_York\n\n**There is no architectural reason the pipeline cannot produce highly optimized native code that matches or exceeds CPython on supported workloads.** There are substantial implementation obligations, and the measurements do not establish how much speed any particular backend would recover. Neither parity across all Python programs nor a particular speedup follows from this ruling.\n\nThis conclusion is stronger than an appeal to “many interpretations”: the tree already specifies register-to-wasm compilation and an LLVM sibling. Both are designs, not demonstrated performance results.\n\nRead-only source commands worked despite the brief’s sandbox warning. I ran no tests, changed nothing, and did not access `collab/`. `docs/design/yin.vm.md` is absent.\n\n**What the architecture permits—and requires**\n\nThe foundational unitarity invariant expressly names **native images** as derived interpretations that may discard facts provided the UAST remains retrievable. Native compilation therefore needs no philosophical exception. Compilation must remain an explicit stream-based transformation, preserve code identity/admission, and retain the source truth. [Architectural foundations](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/datom.world.md:19)\n\nThe difficult obligation is **execution state as observable data**, not merely producing the right Python answer. The register wasm contract requires authoritative supplied snapshots, immutable previously returned snapshots, materialization before state-consuming engine calls and public returns, and preservation of engine changes. Continuations additionally require exact sparse payloads, restore semantics, and qualified refusals. Private execution memory is explicitly permitted as a cache; shared mutable semantic state is not. [Register synchronization contract](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1257)\n\nConsequently:\n\n- **Architectural/logical impossibility:** none established.\n- **Invariant-preserving feasibility:** compile pure regions, specialize with proofs or guards, retain general fallbacks, and reconstruct required state at observation boundaries. This requires serious compiler/runtime work.\n- **Contract-breaking shortcuts:** discarding UAST provenance; treating machine pointers as portable continuations; mutating old snapshots; bypassing stream effects; ignoring runtime rebinding; changing numeric/refusal behavior; or erasing observable cell identities, counters, stores, and suspension boundaries. Those would require changing the named contracts, not merely “optimizing.”\n\nMatching CPython’s answer alone is insufficient evidence of an invariant-preserving implementation.\n\n**Claim 1 — AGREE-WITH-CORRECTIONS**\n\nThe register image is a useful three-address compilation input. The Rust design explicitly identifies fixed register counts, body-local control flow, named operands, and exact live sets as enabling native lowering. It is not already typed, optimized SSA; those further analyses remain work. [LLVM integration rationale](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yin.vm.rust.md:42)\n\nMore importantly, amendments supersede the older peer-identity prose: **A is the code identity; R is a derived integrity checksum**, and the register backend covers a specified semantic fragment. A new optimized artifact must preserve that admission relationship; it cannot silently replace canonical A→R derivation with arbitrary bytes under the same contract. [Controlling amendments](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:5)\n\nThe owner’s question concerns transforming UAST, so the optimizer need not start only after the current prelude expansion. But a higher-level transformation still owes the corresponding semantic and observation-preservation argument.\n\n**Claim 2 — DISSENT on the numerical decomposition; agree with the qualitative diagnosis**\n\nThe supplied data establishes approximately **1,500 register instructions per Python call** and **microseconds per register instruction**. It does **not** independently establish 50× work amplification and 2,500× per-step overhead.\n\nThose factors require a defined CPython work unit and measured CPython work count. Multiplying two plausible factors to reproduce the total ratio is not attribution.\n\nThe sources show several amplification mechanisms:\n\n- Operators invoke guest prelude functions: `py/add` branches through string/list/tuple cases before numeric dispatch.\n- Calls perform function-object lookup and argument binding.\n- Locals and parameters are cells.\n- Returns use continuation-based escapes.\n\nThus “every operator costs ~200 instructions” is neither a source-level constant nor an adequate account of fib’s total work. [Numeric prelude](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1150), [call machinery](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:990), [lowering rules](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:11)\n\nThe interpreter does perform persistent updates and opcode dispatch. But **image validation is admission work**, and continuation validation occurs at restore boundaries; neither is a universal per-instruction validator. The running loop receives `step1` directly, so attributing every instruction’s cost to public protocol dispatch is also unsupported. Closure calls additionally scan bodies and construct register/frame values. [Execution implementation](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:624), [run loop](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:831)\n\nFinally, this checkout’s [benchmark document](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/benchmark.md:1) reports 28.98 ms at n=5,000; it does not document a counted 5,000-instruction primitive tier. Dividing those numbers gives ~5.8 μs **per n-unit**, not independently verified time per instruction.\n\n**Claim 3 — DISSENT as stated**\n\n“AOT alone cannot reach parity” is an unproved performance prediction. The existing LLVM design appropriately says its initial restrictions **bound speedup**, not that they establish a CPython performance ceiling.\n\nLikewise, exceeding CPython does **not logically require tracing or type feedback**. Static proofs, whole-program/prelude analysis, and guarded AOT specialization can remove generic work. Whether they suffice for this fib implementation needs measurement.\n\nThree corrections matter:\n\n1. The integer fast paths are guarded native arithmetic inside host functions—not an already-unboxed guest calling convention.\n2. Linking `py` pins code and profiles; it does not automatically specialize calls or freeze Python globals/function cells.\n3. Unconditional `:load-free` binding is expressly forbidden: resolution is environment → active store → primitives → modules, and runtime definitions can shadow primitives. The LLVM design explicitly allows future **guarded specialization with fallback**. [Translator restrictions](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yin.vm.rust.md:990), [actual resolution](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:115)\n\nThe [linked harness](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:99) publishes/links prelude code and runs existing vector backends. It demonstrates composition, not native call-site optimization.\n\n**Claim 4 — AGREE-WITH-CORRECTIONS**\n\nContinuations, observable effects, and exact integers are genuine constraints. None supplies an impossibility proof.\n\nThe understatement is that continuations are already on the ordinary function-return path: `py/call-ec` allocates a flag cell and captures a continuation. They are not merely an occasional generator or park/resume complication. [Escape implementation](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:338)\n\nThe contract requires reconstructible continuations, not necessarily eager heap allocation for every internal temporary. The wasm design even contemplates future direct calls in proven noncapturing, nonsuspending regions with deoptimization. [Calling-strategy constraints](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yang.wasm.md:243)\n\nEffect equivalence also exceeds “same output order”: store transitions, allocation identities, counters, refusal order, parked state, and public snapshots matter. Cross-host determinism is equivalence under corresponding inputs/profiles/resources, not a promise of identical scheduling under different external events.\n\nFinally, **100,000 bits is this composition’s configured limit**, not an architectural constant. The integer module requires explicit limits and provides exact guarded fast paths with fallback. Small fib values do not exercise bignum arithmetic, although their implementation must retain correct overflow behavior. [Integer kernels](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:216), [limit configuration](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/test/yang/python/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:37)\n\n**Claim 5 — AGREE-WITH-CORRECTIONS**\n\nThe differential-oracle containment is explicit and strong: K0/W0 requires values **and traces**, refusal fixtures, snapshots, continuation transport, and deterministic artifacts. [Conformance kit](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1422)\n\nI found no supporting `latte-kernel` reference in the searched documentation and Yang sources; that analogy is unnecessary.\n\n“One image, JVM bytecode / JS-WASM / Dart AOT” misstates the concrete plan. The documented compiled lane is wasm with an in-instance Rust engine under **Node, Wasmtime, and Wasmer**, with LLVM as a sibling strategy. JVM/Dart remain reference/regression lanes. The `:yang/python` multi-target notes concern parser/frontend availability, not three implemented native code generators. [Wasm targets](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yang.wasm.md:68), [frontend catalog](/home/sto/workspace/datomworld-native-backend-review/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3077)\n\n**The first two slices**\n\n1. **Establish faithful native execution without semantic shortcuts.** Export an oracle fixture for one recursive integer program plus the smallest Python `return` example exercising `py/call-ec`. Run the same admitted image through a native interpreter and mechanically compiled bodies sharing the runtime. Compare step snapshots, calls/captures, stores/counters, errors, and final results. Measure admission separately from execution. This tests how much dispatch/persistent-state overhead compilation actually removes.\n\n2. **Specialize one pinned integer prelude path.** Compile a guarded `py/add` path through `py/arith` to exact small-integer arithmetic, retaining general fallback for changed bindings, other types, and overflow. Preserve single-step behavior and required boundary state. Test rebinding and fallback deliberately, then measure the original fib workload. Do not initially erase local cells or return continuations: those carry additional observable obligations.\n\nFailure to preserve observations falsifies that proposed optimization. Little speedup from the first slice undermines dispatch-centric attribution; little speedup from the second undermines arithmetic-prelude-centric attribution. Neither alone proves native parity impossible.\n\nTo test Claim 2 specifically, collect dynamic counts by prelude operation, calls, captures, cells, and kernel opcode; measure comparable CPython work; and profile allocation/GC, helpers, and dispatch separately. A different work ratio, a nonuniform instruction-cost distribution, or dominant cell/continuation costs falsifies its asserted **50× × 2,500×** explanation.\n\nThe warranted conclusion is feasibility with measurable obligations—not a guarantee that the first native backend will be competitive.\n\n(A) No reason in principle — pipeline transformation to native can reach and exceed cpython; the gap is engineering; state the first slices."}}
